From 5a75ba0fa025dd86b983127eb7ff2f809d43f164 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: "K. S. Ernest (iFire) Lee" <ernest.lee@chibifire.com>
Date: Mon, 21 Sep 2026 16:16:08 -0700
Subject: [PATCH] Stop __wrap_strncmp overwriting the length it was given

CREATE_SYSCALL_STRCMP emits "li a2, 4096" before the ecall. For strcmp that
is a safety cap and correct. strncmp receives its length in a2, so the same
macro discards it and every call compares to the first difference or the NUL
instead of stopping at n.

The unwrapped strncmp on line 68 already uses CREATE_SYSCALL, which leaves a2
alone, so the two spellings of the same function in this file disagreed.

Measured in a guest, through a volatile function pointer so the call is not
inlined and constant-folded:

    strncmp("\"/>", "\"", 1)          returns 47, expected 0
    strncmp("abcXXXX", "abcYYYY", 3)  returns -1, expected 0

47 is '/', the byte at index 1, one past the length requested.

What this breaks downstream is any in-place parser that matches a short
delimiter inside a longer buffer. tinyxml2 finds an attribute's closing quote
with strncmp against a one-character tag, so no XML attribute parses in a
guest at all: <a/> loads and <a b="c"/> does not. That is enough to stop every
MJCF model from loading.
---
 program/cpp/docker/api/native.cpp | 4 +++-
 1 file changed, 3 insertions(+), 1 deletion(-)

diff --git a/program/cpp/docker/api/native.cpp b/program/cpp/docker/api/native.cpp
index 6276ded..94f34cc 100644
--- a/program/cpp/docker/api/native.cpp
+++ b/program/cpp/docker/api/native.cpp
@@ -97,7 +97,9 @@ CREATE_SYSCALL(__wrap_memmove, SYSCALL_MEMMOVE);
 CREATE_SYSCALL(__wrap_memcmp, SYSCALL_MEMCMP);
 CREATE_SYSCALL(__wrap_strlen, SYSCALL_STRLEN);
 CREATE_SYSCALL_STRCMP(__wrap_strcmp, SYSCALL_STRCMP);
-CREATE_SYSCALL_STRCMP(__wrap_strncmp, SYSCALL_STRCMP);
+// strncmp receives its length in a2; CREATE_SYSCALL_STRCMP overwrites a2 with
+// a fixed cap, which is right for strcmp and makes strncmp compare past n.
+CREATE_SYSCALL(__wrap_strncmp, SYSCALL_STRCMP);
 
 extern "C" void *__wrap_malloc(size_t size);
 extern "C" void __wrap_free(void *ptr);
-- 
2.55.0.windows.3

